My mother smiles on the screen.
Then she tells me her side of that morning.
"Diego bought far too much chocolate for the flight at the airport," she says.
I smile at this thought.
"And Sofia took a beautiful photo of us all in the café," she continues.
"Afterwards we stood at the big window."
"We watched the planes together."
She makes a short pause.
"On the way back, Diego said nothing at all."
"He just missed you a lot."
I have not talked about this morning for months.
I sit on my sofa and hold the mobile phone in my hand.
My family is far away in Mexico.
But in this moment I feel very close to them.
"I missed you too," I say quietly.
